Mapping Guidelines

TDC projects primary goal is to replace the DWH with a deltalake hosted in the Databricks environment. 

The complexity in the DWH is quite high and it requires a pragmatic approach when achieving the above goal. Instruction from management is that we 
cannot replace the whole of DWH in one step and should be achieved through couple of stages.
To implement this staged transformation development artifacts that we produce in TDC should align with this staged approach.

This document provides some guidelines for developing our mapping artifacts in line with staged approach so that the business, modellors and developers can 
comprehend mapping document.


TDC Stage One -- Build the deltalake with both source system tables and dwh tables
In this step we follow the following rules when mapping the required attributes.
1) Map required attributes directly to sources tables and fields if there are no transformations and directly accessed in the reports, dashboards etc.
2) Map required attributes directly to sources tables and fields, if the transformations from the source table to intermediary is simple and transformation can be articulated easily.
3) Map required attributes to intermidiary tables if the transformations cannot be easily articulated. 


TDC Stage Two -- Replace the intermediary table references from deltalake.
In this stage, we produce the complex transformations required replace the references of the intermediary tables from deltalake.


Definition:
Required Attributes

These are attributes that a PowerBI report, Dashboard or Machine Learning model expects to source from DWH interfacing tables. Currently these are found in the following
DWH schemas:
source tables (e.g.Swift,Insight)
msit
mstt
bi
mi

Description\Purpose:
Purpose of having this required attribute if it is not obviouse

Intermediary tables:
These are physical tables or views that are available in the DWH database

Spreadsheet Variable:
These are variables that the power bi reports or dashboards directly access from excel spreadsheets.

Transformation:
These are the changes done to source data when materialising them in intermidiary tables (e.g. mstt,mi, bi ) or views (e.g. msit).
Note: any transformation that is done in the power bi reports that is irrelavant to the mapped entity it should be excluded from the mapping 
and if the analysts is of the opinion a transformation in powerbi is worth transferring to deltalake should only do so insultation with modellors and developers.

When describing the tranformations it should be written in plain english without copying and pasting code snippets. 
If the analyst of the opinion code snippets is usefull in articulate his transformation it should by added in the "Code Snippet" column provided it 
does not the reading of the mapping artifact dificult.

Source Table:
DWH table,view or excel sheet where the required attribute can be located.

Source Field:
Field in the where the required attribute can be sourced from source table.

Direct\Derived:
Indicator field, some transformation is required to the source field to produce the required attribute. For these fields there should always 
be accompanied with a transformation logic.
Some examples of these cases are as follows.
a) Required attributes that can be derived by applying simple transformations from source tables to avoid fetching from intermediary tables.
b) Where there is consensus a powerbi transformtion can be performed in the deltalake 

Is PBI logic:
Indicator field to denote the required attribute is derived by transformations applied in powerbi report

Business Comments:
Any Comments on the required attribute that is help full for modelling or development.

Code Snippets:
Any code that is usefull when articulating the transformation

